The [[Medieval History of Burawa]] encompasses the period from the founding of the [[Kingdom of Burawa]] in 403 AD to its defeat of the [[Lake Confederation]] circa 1180. Over the course of approximately eight centuries, the kingdom expanded from a coastal city-state on the [[Mesogean Sea]] to a peninsular power controlling the entirety of [[Southwestern Kaftia]] with the exception of the [[Kingdom of Shedou]] and the [[Kingdom of Orma]]. This expansion was accompanied by the adoption of [[Christianity]], five changes of ruling dynasty, and the development of the [[Hoggaanka]] feudal system.

For a broader treatment of the period across all polities, see '''[[Medieval Southwestern Kaftia]]'''.

== Founding and coastal consolidation (403–500) ==
{{main|Kingdom of Burawa}}

The founding of the [[Kingdom of Burawa]] in 403 AD was a direct response to the economic crisis caused by the collapse of the [[Mesogean League]]. The disintegration of the League's trade networks in the 4th and 5th centuries devastated the port cities of the [[Mesogean Sea|Mesogean]] coast, whose prosperity depended on the export of [[Kushish]] highland iron and the import of Mesogean luxury goods. '''[[Bugad the Great|Bugad I]]''' (r. 403–441), prince of the [[Urtog Dynasty]] of [[Kingdom of Iskhal|Iskhal]], married '''[[Appa of Mazar]]''' of the [[Mazar Clique|House of Masar]], uniting Iskhal's military tradition with Mazar's commercial networks and naval fleet.

The new kingdom incorporated the neighbouring coastal settlements within its first decades. The city-states of [[Aadan]] and [[Qallahere]] to the east and the ports of [[Kaysoor]] and [[Dhuxul]] to the west were brought under Burawi authority through a combination of trade agreements and political pressure. The '''[[Dagaalka Galbeed]]''' (~430–450), a campaign of naval blockade and diplomacy conducted under '''[[Dalgayan I]]''' (r. 441–468), brought the [[Kingdom of Galbeed]] and its capital [[Samara]] into the Burawi sphere. By 500 AD, the kingdom controlled the entire southern coastline and had become the dominant commercial power on the [[Mesogean Sea|Mesogean]] coast.

The capital during this period was '''[[Iskhal]]''', seat of the [[Urtog Dynasty]]. The kingdom was governed through a centralised system of royal governors appointed from the court, a model inherited from Iskhal's city-state administration.

== The Mazar Clique and Christianisation (573–640s) ==
{{main|Burawi Catholic Church}}

The death of '''[[Dalgayan II]]''' (r. 559–573) without a clear heir triggered a succession crisis that brought the [[Mazar Clique|House of Masar]] — the merchant oligarchy that had engineered the founding marriage — to the centre of political power. The Clique placed '''[[Iacob I]]''' (r. 573–612), Dalgayan's great-nephew and a Clique candidate, on the throne.

Iacob I was the first Christian king of Burawa. [[Christianity]] had reached the Kaftian coast via [[Volisanians|Volisanian]] merchants during the reign of '''[[Mahad I]]''' (r. 538–559), and had spread first among the urban merchant class of [[Mazar]] and [[Iskhal]]. Volisanian monks established churches in the harbour districts of both cities. Iacob I's conversion, which may have been encouraged by the Clique's Volisanian trade connections, elevated the faith to a royal religion. He formally established the '''[[Burawi Catholic Church]]''' under royal patronage, modelled on the [[Volisanians|Volisanian]] church hierarchy of bishops, monasteries, and parishes.

The House of Masar governed through two further kings, '''[[Petros I]]''' (r. 612–631) and '''[[Simeon I]]''' (r. 631–643), during which period the Clique remained the dominant political force. The Christianisation of the court and urban population continued, and the first purpose-built church buildings were commissioned in Iskhal and Mazar.

== House Kadim and the golden age (640s–830s) ==

'''[[Marcel I]]''' (r. 643–671), a provincial governor with the support of the [[Burawi Catholic Church]], deposed [[Simeon I]] in a bloodless coup and founded '''[[House Kadim]]'''. The new dynasty displaced the Mazar Clique from its position of political influence, elevating Church officials to advisory roles at court. The Clique's families retained their commercial wealth but ceased to exercise significant political authority.

Marcel I's son, '''[[Teodor I]]''' (r. 671–698), oversaw a period of cultural and intellectual achievement. He '''relocated the capital from [[Iskhal]] to [[Kaysoor]]''', a new city constructed between Iskhal and [[Samara]], in order to establish a seat of royal authority at a distance from the Clique's commercial base. Teodor commissioned the '''Cathedral of the Holy Apostles''' at Kaysoor and encouraged [[Mesogean Sea|Mesogean]] intellectual and artistic exchange. A distinctly Burawi literary tradition in the [[Mesogean script|Echian script]] emerged during his reign.

The Kadim dynasty governed through eight kings over nearly two centuries. The dynasty was ended when '''[[Teodor II]]''' (r. 818–833) was compelled to abdicate by [[Jaamac I]], commander of the eastern garrison. Teodor was subsequently assassinated, likely on Jaamac's orders, though the circumstances remain disputed.

== Conquest of the southern interior ==

=== The Dagaalka Sago (~700s) ===
{{main|Dagaalka Sago}}

The '''Dagaalka Sago''' was the military campaign by which the [[Kingdom of Burawa]] conquered the [[Kingdom of Sago]] during the reign of '''[[Guled I]]''' (r. 698–723). Sago, the most populous kingdom on the peninsula, occupied the fertile [[Dafar River|Dafar]] and [[Hooyal River|Hooyal]] valleys in western [[Burawa]], but its military power was limited by the absence of local iron deposits. Against Burawa's iron-armed professional army, Sago's numerical advantage could not be sustained.

The conquest transformed Burawa from a maritime trading state into a territorial power. The incorporation of Sago's large agricultural population significantly increased the kingdom's food supply and available manpower, and the western river valleys became the principal source of agricultural production for the expanded state. The integration of the former Sago territories into the Burawi administrative system, accomplished under '''[[Andrias I]]''' (r. 723–751) through road construction and the appointment of royal governors, also marked the beginning of the '''[[Burawisation]]''' policy, under which the [[Burawi language]], [[Christianity]], and Burawi legal institutions were extended to conquered populations.

The Dagaalka Sago was a significant development in the formation of Burawi identity. Before the conquest, the kingdom's inhabitants identified primarily as Iskhalites, Mazaris, or Galbeedites; the absorption of a large and culturally distinct population necessitated a broader unifying identity, which subsequently developed around the [[Burawi language]], [[Christianity]], and shared royal institutions.

=== The Northern Conquest (893–925) ===
{{main|Northern Conquest of Burawa}}

The '''Northern Conquest of Burawa''' ([[Burawi language|Burawi]]: ''Dagaalka Waqooyi'') was the campaign by which the [[Kingdom of Burawa]] destroyed the [[Kingdom of Ufyen]] and incorporated the southern highlands into its territory.

The military dynasty of '''[[House Barkhad]]''' — founded by '''[[Jaamac I]]''' (r. 833–861) after the forced abdication of [[Teodor II]] — oriented the entire state toward the conquest of Ufyen. [[Jaamac I]] reorganised the army; his son '''[[Axmed I]]''' (r. 861–893) established forward bases in the middle [[Buran River]] valley. The war was launched in 893 and prosecuted to its conclusion under '''[[Iacob II]]''' (r. 893–931).

The campaign advanced up the river valleys, capturing the ''[[Kaas|kaas]]'' garrisons in succession. The [[Kingdom of Orma]], the Ufyen state's longstanding eastern rival, allied with Burawa during the conquest, attacking the highland kingdom from a second direction. '''[[Hayaan]]''', the last king of the [[Saleebaan Period#The Guri Dynasty|Guri Dynasty]], surrendered after the fall of [[Badalga-Jir]], the Ufyen political capital. The conquest ended nearly two millennia of independent highland rule.

The conquest had extensive consequences for both the kingdom and the conquered highlands. The [[Kushish]] iron mines, the ''kaas'' garrison network, and the sacred mountain of [[Buur Waaq]] all passed into Burawi control. [[Christianity]] was imposed as the state religion in the conquered territory, [[Waaq]] worship was formally suppressed, and the [[Buur Waaq]] summit shrine was abandoned. [[Waaq]] nevertheless persisted in folk practice among the highland population for centuries.

== Governance ==

=== Royal governors and bishops ===

From its founding, Burawa was governed through a centralised system of appointed royal governors, supplemented after Christianisation by [[Burawi Catholic Church|Church]] bishops who served as provincial representatives. This system — inherited from the city-state administration of [[Kingdom of Iskhal|Iskhal]] — was extended to the conquered [[Kingdom of Sago|Sago]] territories and remained the primary mode of governance in the Burawi core throughout the medieval period.

The governor-bishop system was never abolished. After the [[Northern Conquest of Burawa|Northern Conquest]] and the emergence of the [[Family Rule System]] and later the [[Hoggaanka]], the two systems operated '''in parallel''': the central territories — the [[Mesogean Sea|Mesogean]] coast, the [[Buran River]] valley, and the former [[Kingdom of Sago|Sago]] lowlands — continued to be administered by royal governors and Church officials appointed from the capital, while the frontier territories were governed by hereditary [[Hoggaanka|Hoga]] lords. This dual structure created a distinction between the directly governed '''central state''' and the feudal '''Hoga periphery''' that would become a defining tension of the [[Imperial Southwestern Kaftia|Imperial]] period.

=== The Family Rule System (post-925) ===
{{main|Family Rule System}}

After the Northern Conquest, '''[[Iacob II]]''' distributed defensible land in the [[Buran River]] valley and the [[Kushish]] Highlands to approximately twenty to thirty elite warrior families. Many of these grants included captured ''[[Kaas|kaas]]'' forts, whose hilltop positions, stores, and barracks made them natural seats of local power.

These grants were intended as temporary rewards, but the persistent threat of border incursions — [[Dakari people|Dakari]] raids from the north and [[Bariites|Bariite]] pressure from the east — necessitated permanent localised defence. The warrior families settled, and the grants became hereditary. The result was the '''[[Family Rule System]]''' (''Xeerka Qoyska''), an informal feudal arrangement in which warrior nobility and their families held ownership of critical defence points in the kingdom.

The system drew on the Ufyen ''kaas'' model of distributed garrison command, though it differed in one significant respect: ''kaas'' commanders had been centrally appointed and subject to rotation, whereas the Family Rule lords held their territories by hereditary right.

=== The Hoggaanka System (1105) ===
{{main|Hoggaanka}}

In 1105 AD, '''[[Marcel II]]''' (r. 1098–1132) formalised the [[Family Rule System]] into law by royal decree, creating the '''[[Hoggaanka|Hoggaanka System]]''' (''Nidaamka Hoggaanka''). Under the system, the king held the right to create a '''Hogan''' (territory) and assign a '''Hoga''' (lord) to rule it with his family indefinitely. The Hoga was obligated to defend the kingdom and provide men and resources; a Hoga could be removed only with the approval of the '''[[Burawi Catholic Church]]'''. The Hoga ruled his territory without interference and was permitted to expand into unclaimed lands.

The geographic distribution of Hoga power followed a consistent pattern: lords whose territories lay closer to the Burawi core were gradually reabsorbed into the central state, while frontier Hogas in [[Fayeha]], [[Galwa]], and the northern territories accumulated increasing power and autonomy. This structural tension between centre and periphery became a defining feature of the [[Imperial Southwestern Kaftia|Imperial]] period.

== Expansion to the west and north ==

=== The Dagaalka Fayeha (~900s–1000s) ===
{{main|Dagaalka Fayeha}}

The '''Dagaalka Fayeha''' was the western expansion by which the [[Kingdom of Burawa]] absorbed the communities of the [[Western Kaftian Tradition]] in [[Fayeha]] and [[Kijura]]. Unlike the military campaigns against [[Kingdom of Sago|Sago]] and [[Kingdom of Ufyen|Ufyen]], the Western Expansion was more of an administrative absorption than a war. The [[Western Kaftian Tradition]] had no centralised state apparatus and could mount no coordinated resistance. The [[Cowrie|cowrie shell]] trade came under Burawi administration, and [[Christianity]], the [[Burawi language]], and Burawi legal institutions were extended to the absorbed communities.

=== The Dagaalka Harada (~1100s–1180) ===
{{main|Dagaalka Harada}}

The '''Dagaalka Harada''' ([[Burawi language|Burawi]]: "War of the Lake") was the decades-long land campaign by which the [[Kingdom of Burawa]] defeated the [[Lake Confederation]]. [[Marcel II]] launched the war after formalising the [[Hoggaanka]] System, mobilising both the royal professional army and Hoga levies. The campaign was fought entirely overland, without a naval component on the [[Great Kaftian Lake]]. Under '''[[Iacob III]]''' (r. 1132–1161) and '''[[Andrias II]]''' (r. 1161–1185+), the Confederation fragmented and was subdued chiefdom by chiefdom. '''[[Lamido]] Yero''', the last paramount chief, surrendered circa 1180. Burawa also seized the [[Dakari people|Dakari]] coastline east to the [[Ayorin Rainforest]].

== Dynastic politics ==

The kingdom was governed by five royal houses, each transition reflecting a shift in the balance of power between the crown, the merchant class, the [[Burawi Catholic Church|Church]], and the military aristocracy.

